{"id":"loan-many-per-project","title":"Projects with many City loans, and what the published table leaves out","question":"Does the housing office’s published loan table show projects that returned for loan after loan, and can it show whether any loan was repaid?","finding":"The housing office’s only published loan table lists 662 loans and grants to 265 projects, $1.13 billion committed between 1981 and 2015. 96 projects have one loan; 56 have four or more, led by Hunters View (Phase 1) (17 loans, $42.7 million, 2007 to 2013). Whether any of them was repaid cannot be tested from the published records: the table’s only fields are project id, project name, loan id, funding agreement date and amount of loan commitment. It has no borrower, balance, rate, term or repayment, and it stops in 2015.","how":"The DataSF table “Mayor’s Office of Housing and Community Development Grants and Loans”, every row, grouped by project. A project is listed when it has four or more loan or grant commitments; the span is the years between its first and last funding agreement. The two smaller tables the office publishes (home-repair and lead-hazard loans to homeowners) concern private homes and are not listed.","limits":"Several commitments to one project over many years need not mean trouble: the table counts each funding agreement as a row of its own. The table mixes grants with loans and does not say which is which. It was last updated in 2016, so every loan made since, which is most of the money, is absent. What would settle the repayment question: the same table brought up to date with balance, last payment and status for each loan, which the office holds.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"project","t":"Project","type":"text"},{"k":"loans","t":"Loans and grants","type":"int"},{"k":"committed","t":"Committed","type":"usd"},{"k":"first","t":"First agreement","type":"date"},{"k":"last","t":"Latest agreement","type":"date"},{"k":"span","t":"Years between","type":"int"},{"k":"largest","t